WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.438
Step into the shopping
center of the future.

00:00:06.438 --> 00:00:09.333
A world filled with exciting ideas.

00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:11.583
Since they started
popping up in the 1950s,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.830
malls have been the quintessential

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:16.900
American shopping experience.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.920
Bustling temples dedicated to the wonders

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.000
and excesses of retail, food
service and entertainment,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.660
the rise of the mall coincided

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.530
with the growth of the suburbs.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.800
The heyday of the malls
really is the 1970s,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.150
sometimes referred to as
the malling of America.

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:35.720
They really thrived at providing

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.820
some sort of public gathering space,

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.330
some sort of social life,

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:45.880
in addition to actually
giving people places to shop.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.110
Over the past few decades before then,

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.280
lots of developers were building malls,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.870
and it kind of coincided
with the growth of highways.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.210
So as more people started traveling

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.090
and interstate started being built,

00:00:58.090 --> 00:00:59.940
developers really wanted to put something

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.343
to attract people to stop.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.269
Take a look

00:01:07.269 --> 00:01:08.650
at an average American mall today however,

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:11.000
and you'll see a very different picture.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.840
Closed stores and empty hallways

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.370
became the norm in many malls

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.380
even before COVID-19 delivered them

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.513
another set of problems in 2020.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:21.600
So what happened?

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.453
This is the story of the rise
and fall of American malls.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.140
When malls worked best,
they survived and thrived

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:42.970
thanks to their anchor stores.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:43.850
Those are the stores

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.690
that are on the edges of the malls

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.410
and that are outward
facing to the parking lot.

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:49.690
So if you're a shopper,

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.350
you're gonna walk
through one of these big,

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:52.940
usually a department store.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:57.140
So if you're thinking about
Macy's or Sears or JCPenney,

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.260
those are called the anchor stores.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.780
So once shoppers are done shopping there

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.320
maybe they wander to the
middle part of the mall,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.350
shopping at the other apparel shops

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.050
or even going to buy some food.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.140
But you really had to have a reason

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.040
for people to shop in the first place.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.090
Because anchor is
bringing all those people,

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.230
they tend to pay very little rent.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:23.400
Around 80% of income from all owners

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.160
come from the inline
stores inside the mall.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.520
Over the past few decades,

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.040
malls have been facing
challenges from all sides.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.060
The first being, there's
just too many of them.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.450
As early as in the 90s,

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:38.340
the malls were beginning
to compete with each other.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.680
We have a lot more

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.030
retail square footage
per capita than Canada.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.550
We have only seven times

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:48.000
more retail square footage
per capita than Europe.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.860
After the 2008 financial crisis

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.120
lowered retail spending across the board,

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.210
malls and especially
their department stores

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.210
also had to contend

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.790
with a new challenge
that was growing fast.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:00.680
Malls started to suffer in the early 2000s

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.250
with the rise of online shopping.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.170
Suddenly they didn't have
that captive audience

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:09.110
who had to go to the
mall to buy something.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.520
You didn't have to get up,

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.160
get in your car drive
all the way to the mall

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.420
and then see a bunch of other people.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.180
You can just do it from
the comfort of your home.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:18.300
Department stores overall

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:21.390
have become less relevant over the years.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.120
Many have fallen totally off

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.400
and that's Sears, JCPenney.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.470
Several bankruptcies in this space

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:31.500
have really hurt the industry.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.360
But as department stores goes

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.400
so does the mall oftentimes.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.940
So if department stores
start closing locations

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.990
that means they're usually leaving malls.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.023
This is the Greenbrier mall
in Southwest Atlanta, Georgia.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.730
It's in many ways emblematic

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.873
of the problems facing malls today.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.090
It's been there since 1965.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.390
So for more than half of a century

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:01.130
it's been serving this area of Atlanta

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:02.950
that's predominantly black

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.293
and also as middle-class neighborhood.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.140
It started with anchor stores
that were department stores.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.450
So you had JCPenney and then Riches

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:14.116
which was later bought by Macy's.

00:04:14.116 --> 00:04:16.680
So you had these two national chains.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.460
It had the first Chick-Fil-A in a mall

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:20.970
like that type of format.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.030
So when you think about
the history of malls

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:26.060
like Greenbrier had like
the perfect template

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.220
but now Macy's will be exiting the mall

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.130
meaning that the mall will
be left without an anchor

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:35.210
which is vital to
attracting other shoppers

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.090
to come into the stores.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.380
Greenbrier then because
it's kind of an example

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:43.410
or a test for all these other
middling malls across America

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.130
about what do you do when
these large department stores

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.610
as a business decision on their end

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.250
are trying to just stay
in the high quality malls,

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.380
how does a middling mall survive?

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:55.763
What's their next strategy?

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.110
Greenbrier is a textbook example

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.880
of how closely the deterioration

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.210
of the middle class in the US

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.143
is tied to the health of its malls.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.370
The median income for the surrounding area

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:13.460
has reduced by $6,905
over the past 15 years

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:17.240
to a total of $20,756 per year.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:20.040
And yet residents and store
owners hope that Greenbrier Mall

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:21.840
will see better days.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.190
Malls are made up of the people

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.430
and communities that help fuel them.

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:28.440
Greenbrier is still a place
that people go to and cherish

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.780
even if on paper it
doesn't have the elements

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.890
that make it a thriving mall.

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:36.420
Well, a lot of the existing constituents

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.760
and residents there feel in many respects

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:42.990
that they worked hard their whole lives

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:46.530
to be able to experience
the American dream

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.300
The single family, suburban
house in a leafy yard

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:54.903
near a big mall that had a great Macy's.

00:05:55.940 --> 00:06:00.940
They're very reluctant to see
that taken away from them.

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:09.290
Now that Macy's has gone,

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.450
Greenbriar may try to take
inspiration from what many malls

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:14.250
were doing in the years
following the financial crisis

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:17.250
focusing not on shopping but experiences.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.390
That was a trend we were seeing

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:22.470
in 2016 up into the pandemic pretty much.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:23.760
And by experiences

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.850
I mean everything as
traumatic as a waterpark

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:31.050
or hosting like concerts
to have people wanna come

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.340
other than just to buy stuff.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:35.510
Having Instagrammable moments,

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:37.190
making sure your store is pretty

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:38.890
that people wanna take a photo

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:41.040
and post it on Instagram or Twitter.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.950
Historically the mall has always
been a place for teenagers.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.760
You like to hang out, see your friends

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.130
and also kind of be seen at the mall.

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:50.160
And that hasn't completely changed.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.830
Like before the pandemic,
about 95% of gen Zs,

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.550
so today's teenagers had gone to a mall

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.230
in a three month period.

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:00.830
So teenagers are still going to the mall

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:03.920
and they've always been key
to having thriving malls

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.960
because they're coming
into their spending power.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:08.560
And once you went over teens

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.190
hopefully you're really winning

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:11.883
over a loyal customer for life.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:18.760
But thanks to lock downs
across the country,

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:22.093
most malls at one point or
another were shuttered in 2020.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.770
That means no one using
the multi-billion dollar

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:30.260
American Dream malls indoor
ski slope, no one going

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:33.750
to the King of Prussia Mall's
indoor skydiving attraction.

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:35.560
Basically the pandemic gave malls

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:36.970
what they didn't need.

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:38.560
Another problem.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:40.490
What would your net income

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:41.900
or net operating income

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:45.636
have been in 2022 without COVID

00:07:45.636 --> 00:07:48.040
and what is it now in a post COVID world?

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:51.580
So, our view is it was a roughly 10% hit

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:54.053
to net income over that period.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:56.840
That's Vince Tibone from the analytics

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:59.590
from Green Street Advisors,
which rates American models

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:02.030
from A to D just like a report card.

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:03.970
And if malls did have a report card,

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.133
they wouldn't exactly make honor roll.

00:08:07.531 --> 00:08:10.430
Here in the US, out of
1000 malls in the country,

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:15.170
there are roughly 250 malls
we grade A- or better.

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:17.170
The B and C quality malls

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:20.000
which is the vast majority
of the malls by count

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.950
we think are on a negative trajectory.

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:24.130
And it's gonna depend on

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:27.400
a lot of the local dynamics
ownership decisions

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.620
around how much money do
you invest in the property

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.350
will determine whether this mall

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:36.350
can survive as retail or
rapidly melting ice cube.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:38.570
While there have been a few bankruptcies

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:40.130
among real estate investment trusts

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:43.260
that own mostly B to D
malls in the past few years,

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.493
A models really are doing just fine.

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:52.000
Some of the most
productive shopping centers

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.440
in the country are super high end.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:57.310
So I'm talking about Bal Harbor Shops

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:59.520
down in Miami, Florida,

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.800
there is Americana Manhasset
in Long Islands, Gold Coast,

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.700
the Grove in Los Angeles.

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.730
These are places that
have all high end stores

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:11.170
and have done quite well.

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:14.220
A lot of that has to do with
efficiency of their property.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.980
So their sales per square
foot are really high

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:21.620
but also they've invested
a lot in their actual mall

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:23.030
'cause it has to look beautiful,

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:25.096
it has to have all the services

00:09:25.096 --> 00:09:28.450
that a wealthy shopper expects.

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:29.780
So what will the future hold

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:31.140
for less sought after malls

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:33.003
that can't revive their golden years?

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:37.610
For many of these malls,
it will mean closure

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:40.060
and then hopefully conversion.

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:43.580
If they can't get a new use to come in,

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:47.010
it's expensive but you
can just demolish it.

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:49.290
And there are still quite a lot of sites

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.160
just sitting waiting.

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:53.990
Amazon has been picking
up more and more of those

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:56.250
building their big fulfillment centers.

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:59.500
Also, a lot of communities
see this as an opportunity

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:03.830
to redevelop, reinhabit this dead mall

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:06.850
to help that community meet the challenges

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:09.750
it was never originally designed for.

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:14.750
The number one reuse of an
existing mall is as workspace.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:18.770
It's actually pretty easy to
just throw some carpeting down,

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:20.370
cut a few more windows

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:22.770
and turn it into office space.

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:25.030
Sort of number two reuse of malls

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:29.050
is either medical,
healthcare or education.

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:33.250
Lots of great examples
of community colleges

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:37.213
all the way down to elementary
schools moving into malls.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:43.070
And then the third strategy
that I wish happened more

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:47.640
is actually just demolishing
them all and regreening.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:51.090
Either reconstructing the
wetlands that used to be there

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.410
And that frankly we never
should have built on

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:54.370
in the first place

00:10:55.281 --> 00:10:58.940
or providing community parks and gardens.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:01.660
While most analysts say post
pandemic retail shopping

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:04.080
could bring some life back
into the American mall,

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:06.290
many are not long for this world.

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:07.410
Depending on where you live,

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:09.990
It might be time to make one last visit,

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:14.263
grab some food, do some
shopping and say farewell.

